<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Arkwright</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1732">1732</a>–<a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard Arkwright was an English inventor and industrialist, often called the father of the factory system. He patented the water frame, a spinning machine driven by water power, in 1769, and from 1771 built a water-powered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mill at Cromford in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>, with housing for its workers. His mills were widely copied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and abroad, and he was knighted in 1786.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-arkwright.jpg" alt="Richard Arkwright" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mather Brown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Richard_Arkwright_by_Mather_Brown_1790.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Richard Arkwright was born in Preston, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, on 23 December 1732, the youngest of seven surviving children of Thomas, a tailor, and Sarah. His parents could not afford to send him to school, and a cousin, Ellen, taught him to read and write. He was apprenticed to a barber at Kirkham and began his working life as a barber and wig-maker, setting up a shop at Churchgate in Bolton in the early 1760s. There he invented a waterproof dye for periwigs, and its income later funded his prototype <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> machinery. He married Patience Holt in 1755; she died the next year, and in 1761 he married Margaret Biggins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1768 Arkwright and the clockmaker John Kay returned to Preston and worked on a spinning machine, patented in 1769, that twisted thread with wooden and metal cylinders instead of human fingers. In 1775 he patented a carding engine, an improvement on a machine Lewis Paul had invented in 1748. For capital he formed a partnership with the hosiery manufacturers Jedediah Strutt and Samuel Need, and in 1771 the partners built a mill at Cromford that handled both carding and spinning and employed 200 people. A second, larger mill followed there in 1776, with others at Bakewell, Wirksworth and elsewhere.</p><p>Because Cromford's own population could not supply the labour, Arkwright brought in workers and housed them in cottages beside the mill. Work ran in two 13-hour shifts, the gates were shut at 6 am and 6 pm, and towards the end of his tenure nearly two thirds of the 1,150 employees were children. His grand patent of 1775 met hostility, and in 1785 a court case he had begun in 1781 was settled against him because his specifications were deficient; the court had also heard claims that the frame was the work of John Kay or of Thomas Highs. His mill at Birkacre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> was destroyed in the anti-machinery riots of 1779.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Arkwright served as High Sheriff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a> and was knighted in 1786. He built Willersley Castle at Cromford, but a fire in 1792 delayed its completion and he never lived in it. He died at Rock House, Cromford, on 3 August 1792, aged 59, leaving a fortune of £500,000. The Cromford mill complex is now part of the Derwent Valley Mills World Heritage Site and is owned by the Arkwright Society. The Arkwright Scholarships Trust, founded in 1991, awards scholarships to students of engineering and technical design.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richard Arkwright” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 1760s the Industrial Revolution began, with James Hargreaves’s spinning jenny, <b>Richard Arkwright</b>’s water frame and factories, James Watt’s improved steam engine, patented in 1769, and canals such as the Bridgewater Canal of 1761.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Preston, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Cromford, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.76,-2.7&amp;year=1771" aria-label="Preston on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4490 317 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Richard Arkwright"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4940 587h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4940 587h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.76,-2.7&amp;year=1771">What was here in 1771? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
